Routing and Addressing Troubleshooting (Day 5)

Three more faults from the same lab: a wrong IP and mask, a wrong default gateway, and a router sub-interface that was shut down. Same as before, I broke one thing, fixed it and checked it worked before moving on.

                                             Fault 2 - Wrong IP address and mask on PC-HR

Symptom:

PC-HR could not reach its gateway (192.168.20.1). Pinging it gave request timed out on all 4 packets, 100 percent loss.

What I did to break it:

On PC-HR under Desktop > IP Configuration I set the IP to 192.168.2.95 and the mask to 255.255.255.128.

Commands used to diagnose:

C:\> ipconfig /all

Then I worked out the subnet by hand. With a /25 mask, 192.168.2.95 is in the network 192.168.2.0/25, which covers 192.168.2.1 to 192.168.2.126.

Root cause:

Two mistakes at once. The address was in the wrong network (192.168.2.x instead of 192.168.20.x) and the mask was /25 instead of /24. The gateway 192.168.20.1 is nowhere near 192.168.2.0/25, so the PC thought it was not on the same subnet and never even tried to ARP for it.

Fix:

On PC-HR I changed the settings to

IP address:      192.168.20.10
Subnet mask:     255.255.255.0
Default gateway: 192.168.20.1

Verification:

C:\> ping 192.168.20.1

Pinging 192.168.20.1 with 32 bytes of data:
Reply from 192.168.20.1: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=255
Reply from 192.168.20.1: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=255
Reply from 192.168.20.1: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=255
Reply from 192.168.20.1: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=255

Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 4, Lost = 0 (0% loss)
